  "ai_show_review": {
    "summary": "Acoustic gems, soaring electric set and rare piano on To Lay Me Down",
    "blurb": "The Fillmore East show opens with a tight acoustic set that highlights folk staples and guest mandolin/piano textures. Jerry’s surprising piano work on “To Lay Me Down” adds a solemn beauty, while the second set erupts into high‑energy classics like “Not Fade Away”, “Cumberland Blues” and “New Speedway Boogie”. Overall it’s a strong performance with several memorable moments.",
    "review": "On September 20, 1970 at the Fillmore East, the Dead delivered an unforgettable two‑set marathon that still feels fresh. Set 1 is a folk‑rock showcase – Uncle John’s Band, Ripple and a beautifully rendered “To Lay Me Down” where Jerry Garcia slips onto piano alongside Tom Constanten’s mournful keys, creating a rare, solemn moment. The acoustic momentum carries through Truckin’ and the blue‑grass‑tinged “Cumberland Blues”. Set 2 flips the switch: classic rockers like Casey Jones flow into the seamless China Cat → I Know You Rider segue, then the band rides high on an explosive “Not Fade Away”, a thunderous “New Speedway Boogie” and a tight “Easy Wind” solo from Bob. The Caution/Feedback jam adds the typical late‑‘70 experimental edge. Guest contributions and the interplay between Jerry’s guitar, Phil’s steady bass (even adding electric depth to the acoustic side), and Pigpen’s organ round out an evening that captures the band at a creative peak.",
